  "text": "Hagar named God herself, out loud, in the desert. Genesis sixteen. Hagar is Sarai's Egyptian handmaid, and she is given to Abram, and when she conceives, the text says her mistress was despised in her eyes, and Sarai deals harshly with her. So Hagar runs. Pregnant, alone, into the wilderness. And the angel of the Lord finds her by a fountain of water in the wilderness, by the way to Shur. And he calls her by name and by station. Hagar, Sarai's maid, whence camest thou? And whither wilt thou go? Two questions. Where have you come from, and where are you going. She answers only the first one, because she does not have an answer to the second. Then she is told she will bear a son, and to call his name Ishmael, because the Lord hath heard thy affliction. And then she does something that, as far as the text shows us, nobody in Scripture had done before her. She calls the name of the Lord that spake unto her, Thou God seest me. She names God. A runaway foreign slave woman in a desert, and she is the one who gets to say it.",
